> > but I want to ascend one anyway. Having tried and failed
> > three times, I think there's a fundamental flaw in my
> > approach. My biggest questions for ascended barbarians are:

> > 1. How do you circumvent the barbarian's weaknesses in
> > ranged combat?  I've tried bow/+6 poisoned arrows and
> > hoarding pets in Sporkhack. For some reason, my pet purple
> > worms seemed to die off far too quickly.  Poisoned arrows
> > worked nicely versus priests and soldiers, but I didn't use
> > them on a regular basis.

> Couldn't find any daggers to throw? *always* the best option
> if you can become skilled (or even sometimes basic) in them.

Not always.  If you can become expert in bow, and find a lot of
arrows, that can be even better than daggers.  (At least in the
later stages, when you've blessed them and enchanted them.)

Still, for barbarians, yes.  You can only get to basic with
dagger, but you can't really do any better with anything else.
(I've ascended a Barbarian wielding nothing but Magicbane and
throwing daggers from the castle on.  So it's quite doable.)

> > 2. Do you #twoweapon or use a two-hander such as Cleaver?
> > In Sporkhack, it's nearly impossible to hit many enemies,
> > such as arch- liches, Rodney, and named &, with basic
> > #twoweapon or even basic single-weapon, so I've been using
> > the Cleaver. However, when Cleaver got cursed in Moloch's
> > sanctum, I rethought my approach.

> So you get skilled in a good weapon early. Surely as a barb,
> you can get skilled in the three "big" swords (broad long and
> sabre), axe and dagger?

You can't get above basic in dagger or sabre.  You really do
want either a long sword or a broadsword at the beginning,
preferably two weaponing.  Once your armors up where it should
be, however, you've max'ed out your luck and have a reasonable
XL, the actual weapon becomes a lot less important.  (At least,
that's my impression---although I'll admit my first ascension
was a Valk twoweaponing Grayswandir and a katana, both fully
enchanted.)

